# secrets — never commit; .env.example carries the placeholders
.env

# derived — regenerate with `make dataset-materialize`
data/materialized/*
!data/materialized/README.md

# Experiment output. THE SCORES ARE COMMITTED; the generations are not.
#
# An external review found the decisive gap: with every run ignored, not one number in
# the five reports could be recomputed by anyone but the author on one laptop. A repo
# whose entire argument is "verify the instrument" was itself unverifiable. So:
#
#   metrics.json      1.0 MB total — every leaderboard, cost and headline figure
#   predictions.jsonl  39 MB total — per-item scores, so every separation test,
#                                    rank-stability curve and holdout can be rerun
#   outputs/           COMMITTED, 7.3 MB — see below
#
# Checked before committing: predictions.jsonl carries no article text. `_meta` holds
# provider metadata, a predicted label, a list of document ids, or Few-NERD entity
# spans (CC BY-SA 4.0, redistributable with attribution). No summary, no source
# sentence, no abstract.
# OUTPUTS ARE COMMITTED TOO, and the earlier decision not to rested on two wrong
# numbers of mine. I estimated "128 MB of copyrighted derivatives" from a `du` that
# counted directory blocks and the excluded dev runs. Measured per example:
#
#   AG News      0.0 MB   single words -- "World"
#   DBpedia      0.1 MB   single words -- "Artist"
#   NER          1.3 MB   entity spans from Few-NERD, CC BY-SA 4.0
#   retrieval    4.2 MB   document ids, no corpus text at all
#   summarisation 1.7 MB  model-generated summaries  <- the only real question
#
# 7.3 MB total, and four of the five carry no copyright question whatsoever.
#
# The summarisation outputs are the judgement call: ~60-word model paraphrases of
# CNN/DailyMail articles. They are model OUTPUT rather than corpus text, the dataset is
# Apache-2.0 tagged on HuggingFace, and distributing system outputs is what every
# summarisation shared task does. Against that: they are derived from copyrighted news.
# Committed, because `rescore.py` reads this directory and without it a reader can check
# our numbers but cannot re-derive them under a changed scorer -- which is the single
# most useful thing this harness does, and was the largest remaining reproducibility
# hole. To reverse just this one, add: data/runs/cnn_*/outputs/
data/runs/*/*
!data/runs/*/metrics.json
!data/runs/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs/*/outputs
!data/runs/*/outputs/**
!data/runs/README.md

# ...but NOT these two, which exist for crash recovery and are redundant once a run
# exits cleanly:
#   _rows.jsonl  the per-item rows, appended during the pass so a crash leaves them.
#                predictions.jsonl is the same data, written on a clean exit.
#   run.json     the run's identity, written before the first item so a crashed run
#                can still say which arm produced its outputs. metrics.json carries
#                the same fields and more.
# Committing either would duplicate every prediction row for no reader.
data/runs/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl
data/runs/*/run.json

# And the temp files `write_text_atomic` creates next to their target. A crash between
# mkstemp and os.replace leaves one behind, and `!data/runs/*/outputs/**` above would
# otherwise commit it. Found by external review.
data/runs/*/outputs/*.tmp
data/runs/*/outputs/.*.tmp

# EXCEPT the 72 summarisation DEV runs (cnn_dailymail_20, three repeats each). They
# predate `_portable_id` and record an absolute adapter path containing a username and
# a former project name. They are excluded rather than edited: rewriting a field inside
# a finished run would leave `fingerprint.hash`, which was computed over the original
# bytes, describing content that no longer exists -- a worse failure than withholding
# the file. No MEASUREMENT run is affected; every number in the reports comes from the
# n=200/280 runs, which are committed. Regenerate these with
#   make experiment-run CONFIG=../examples/summarization-cnn-dailymail/configs/arm_<x>.yaml ARGS="--repeat 3"
#
# THE OUTPUTS LINE BELOW WAS MISSING AND IT MATTERED. These four patterns excluded the
# dev runs' metrics and predictions, and then `!data/runs/*/outputs/**` above re-admitted
# their outputs behind that exclusion -- so 144 of these directories were committed as
# outputs ONLY: 2,880 files of CNN/DailyMail paraphrases with no metrics.json to say what
# they are, which no tool in this repo can read. All of the copyright exposure of the
# summarisation outputs and none of the reproducibility benefit, which is the worst of
# both trades. Found by external review; the exclusion is now written per DIRECTORY so a
# future force-include cannot slip underneath it again.
data/runs/cnn_*_v[12]_2026092[56]T*_r[0-9]/
data/runs/cnn_*_v[12]_2026092[56]T*_r[0-9]/**
# The ONE worked example that ships, so a fresh checkout sees a walked loop instead of an
# empty directory: sources -> dataset -> reference -> run -> baseline. It is also what keeps
# the tree self-consistent — data/baselines/ is committed and V6 requires each baseline's
# source run to exist, so a committed baseline must point at a committed run. Your own runs
# stay ignored by default; commit them if you want them, that is your call.
!data/runs/demo_v1_20260925T070923Z/
!data/runs/demo_v1_20260925T070923Z/**

# judge panel output
out/

.venv
__pycache__/

# ── third-party corpus content: NEVER committed to this public repo ──────────
# CNN/DailyMail article text and its human-written highlights belong to the dataset's
# publishers, not to us. The example ships a DOWNLOAD RECIPE instead — see
# ../summarization-cnn-dailymail/fetch.py and its README — so anyone can reproduce the
# tree locally without us redistributing anything.
#
# The synthetic demo items (data/sources/item_0*.txt) are OURS and stay tracked; that is
# why these rules name the dataset directory rather than the whole of sources/.
# Convention for the next example: real corpora live in data/sources/<dataset_id>/ and
# get a line here.
# Globbed by slice size, not written out per size: `fetch.py --n 200` names its dataset
# cnn_dailymail_200, and a corpus whose exclusion depends on someone remembering to add a
# line is one forgotten line away from being redistributed.
data/sources/cnn_dailymail_*/
data/references/gold/cnn_dailymail_*/*
data/references/silver/cnn_dailymail_*/*

# AG News, same rule and a weaker licence than CNN/DailyMail's: the dataset card says
# `unknown` and the corpus's own description says non-commercial research use. We
# redistribute nothing -- examples/classification-ag-news/fetch.py downloads a slice to
# whoever runs it. These lines go in BEFORE the first fetch, not after: the window between
# downloading a third-party corpus and remembering to ignore it is the window in which it
# gets committed to a public repo.
data/sources/ag_news_*/
# Each fetcher's map of item to source row (examples/_shared/hf_rows.Sources): what
# `evh dataset-profile` reads; the committed record of it is the profile (data/profiles/).
data/sources/*.rows.json
data/references/gold/ag_news_*/*
data/references/silver/ag_news_*/*

# DBpedia-14. Licence here is actually clean (CC-BY-SA 3.0 + GFDL, inherited from
# Wikipedia) -- unlike AG News -- and it is still not ours to redistribute. Same rule,
# same reason, same timing: these lines land before the first fetch.
data/sources/dbpedia_*/
data/references/gold/dbpedia_*/*
data/references/silver/dbpedia_*/*

# Few-NERD. CC BY-SA 4.0 -- the cleanest licence of the four corpora here -- and still
# not ours to redistribute. Same rule, same timing: in before the first fetch.
data/sources/few_nerd_*/
data/references/gold/few_nerd_*/*
data/references/silver/few_nerd_*/*

# SummEval (MIT; the articles are CNN's). Its items carry the article, 16 machine summaries
# and the human references, and its gold is the expert scores -- none of it ours to
# redistribute. (These lines landed just after the first fetch, before any commit.)
data/sources/summeval_*/
data/references/gold/summeval_*/*
data/references/silver/summeval_*/*

# EVERY OTHER runs directory stays out, BY DEFAULT RATHER THAN BY NAME. Smoke passes,
# tuning sweeps, superseded runs, self-test fixtures, one-off scopes: working
# artifacts, not the record.
#
# This used to list them one per line, which is default-ALLOW wearing a deny's clothes:
# any directory nobody thought to add was tracked. `data/runs-resumesrc/` -- a fixture
# a self-test creates and deletes -- was committed that way, 7 files of it, because it
# happened to exist when someone ran `git add -A`. I then did the same thing to myself
# while fixing it, sweeping an in-progress re-run into a commit. Found by external
# review.
#
# So: deny the whole shape FIRST, then name the two that genuinely ship. Order is the
# whole trick: in gitignore the LAST matching rule wins, so the re-allows have to sit
# below the deny. My first version put them above it, which left the deny winning --
# the two directories stayed tracked only because git ignores nothing already tracked,
# and adding any NEW file to either (this README, for one) silently failed.
data/runs-*/

# --- re-allowed, and only these ---------------------------------------------------

# data/runs-repeats/ — second runs of an arm already measured. They live apart from
# data/runs because two runs sharing a config_id make every loader pick one by
# filesystem order: `check_report_claims` answered 29/29 on APFS and 28/29 on ext4 off
# exactly that, and `leaderboard` silently printed the MEAN of the two as though it
# were a measurement. Kept, because they are the only evidence this repo has about
# between-run variance. Found by external review.
!data/runs-repeats/
data/runs-repeats/*/*
!data/runs-repeats/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-repeats/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-repeats/*/outputs
!data/runs-repeats/*/outputs/**
data/runs-repeats/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl
!data/runs-repeats/README.md

# data/runs-linux/ — every local arm re-run on ONE machine, back to back, idle. The
# record in data/runs/ mixes two hosts (a 12-core Intel Mac for the original arms, a
# 4-core Linux container for the six added on 2026-09-30), so latencies across that
# boundary are not comparable; these are. Same config_ids as data/runs, which is why they
# cannot live there. Point EVAL_RUNS_DIR here to compare local arms' speed.
!data/runs-linux/
data/runs-linux/*/*
!data/runs-linux/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-linux/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-linux/*/outputs
!data/runs-linux/*/outputs/**
data/runs-linux/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl
!data/runs-linux/README.md

# data/runs-adjudicated/ — AG News re-scored ($0) against gold/ag_news_200_adj1, the gold with
# 17 labels changed by one reader (examples/classification-ag-news/adjudication.json, the
# committed record): the report's main caveat, as a group on the goal page.
!data/runs-adjudicated/
data/runs-adjudicated/*/*
!data/runs-adjudicated/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-adjudicated/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-adjudicated/*/outputs
!data/runs-adjudicated/*/outputs/**
data/runs-adjudicated/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl

# data/runs-rescored/<scorer>/<source>/ — re-scores (evh rescore, RFC-022): the stored outputs read again by
# another scorer, $0, the scorer by its code, the source the runs directory read. The one place the tool
# designates, so a new scorer never needs a block of its own here.
!data/runs-rescored/
data/runs-rescored/*/*/*/*
!data/runs-rescored/*/*/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-rescored/*/*/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-rescored/*/*/*/outputs
!data/runs-rescored/*/*/*/outputs/**
data/runs-rescored/*/*/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl

# data/runs-silver/ — the CNN arms re-scored ($0) against silver/cnn_dailymail_200, the frontier
# arm's summaries: a silver group beside gold on the goal page, where silver's family bias shows.
# Their outputs are the same bytes as the measured runs' in data/runs.
!data/runs-silver/
data/runs-silver/*/*
!data/runs-silver/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-silver/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-silver/*/outputs
!data/runs-silver/*/outputs/**
data/runs-silver/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl

# data/runs-judged/<scorer>/ — the CNN summaries re-measured by LLM judges (ADR-0001), each run
# projected from the stored verdicts in data/judge-verdicts/ by `judge-score --no-calls` ($0).
# Committed so the catalog shows a judge's group beside gold; rebuilt byte for byte from the
# verdicts, which are the evidence.
!data/runs-judged/
data/runs-judged/*/*/*
!data/runs-judged/*/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-judged/*/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-judged/*/*/outputs
!data/runs-judged/*/*/outputs/**
data/runs-judged/*/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl

# EXCEPT data/runs-pair/, WHICH WAS NEVER A WORKING ARTIFACT. It holds the four runs
# behind the pre-registered prediction -- the single most-advertised result in this
# repo, that swapping only the first stage would move the pipeline from 0.74 to
# 0.786-0.797, which came back at 0.7891. I filed it under "the first-stage pair" as
# scratch and ignored the whole directory, so the one number a reader is most likely to
# want to check was the one number with no committed evidence. Found by external review.
!data/runs-pair/
data/runs-pair/*/*
!data/runs-pair/*/metrics.json
!data/runs-pair/*/predictions.jsonl
!data/runs-pair/*/outputs
!data/runs-pair/*/outputs/**
data/runs-pair/*/outputs/_rows.jsonl

# Any other EVAL_RUNS_DIR: smoke passes, timing probes, throwaway scopes. Ignored for the
# same reason, plus one the last sweep learned the hard way -- an untracked directory under
# the harness makes the tree DIRTY, and every run launched beside it records
# `harness.dirty: true` and cannot be reproduced from its own fingerprint.
data/runs-smoke*/
data/runs-tune*/
# GLOBBED, not enumerated, for the reason the corpus rules above are: a rule that needs
# someone to remember a new line is one forgotten line away from committing 240 run
# artifacts. `data/runs-tune/` was exactly that -- the comment above already said "any
# other EVAL_RUNS_DIR" and only `runs-smoke*` was actually listed.
!data/runs/

# BEIR SciFact. CC BY-SA 4.0, and still not ours to redistribute. Same rule, same timing:
# in before the first fetch.
data/sources/scifact_*/
data/references/gold/scifact_*/*
data/references/silver/scifact_*/*

# ── the CORPUS, which is a new kind of thing in this tree ────────────────────
# Retrieval is the first task here whose answer is not computable from the item alone. An
# item is a QUERY; the documents it must find are a separate 5,183-abstract corpus that
# every arm indexes and no arm is scored on. It is not `sources/` (not one file per item),
# not `references/` (not ground truth), and not `materialized/` (not derived from our
# inputs) — so it gets its own directory, and the same never-redistribute rule.
#
# A corpus that is not fingerprinted is the worst silent failure available to this repo:
# two runs over the SAME queries against DIFFERENT corpora would otherwise look identical
# in every recorded field. `corpus_sha256` is in the arm fingerprint for that reason, and
# the manifest that carries it is the one tracked file here.
data/corpora/*
!data/corpora/README.md

# `make demo` writes three fresh runs on every invocation, so a newcomer's first command
# left an untracked tree. The demo runs already committed stay tracked (ignore rules do not
# apply to tracked files); new ones are scratch.
data/runs/demo_v1_*/

# derived: the family test's answers, keyed on its exact inputs (scripts/family_test.py)
.cache/

# packaging by-products (pip install / python -m build)
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Third-party data fetched by scripts (e.g. validate_judge_goyal.py); never committed.
data/external/

# the built catalog -- regenerate with `make catalog`
.build/

# A gold reference not redistributed keeps its manifest (checksums, how to rebuild it): validate V16.
!data/references/gold/*/manifest.json
# A silver reference made from a committed run keeps its manifest the same way: its texts are
# that run's outputs, rebuilt with `evh reference-create --from-run <run_id>` (the manifest
# names the run).
!data/references/silver/*/manifest.json

# The triage fleet's decisions (examples/triage-fleet/fetch.py): published on Hugging Face
# (chipi-nbgd/orrery-triage, CC-BY-4.0) and fetched by revision; git holds their ids and hashes.
data/sources/orrery_triage_*/
data/sources/orrery_triage_v1.hf/
# A capture's records are the captured content; capture.json (ids and hashes) is what git holds.
data/captures/*/records/
data/sources/fleet_dedup_*/
data/sources/fleet_dedup_v1.hf/
